WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.080
Jeremy Foley, Filmmaker/Actor/Screenwriter: After&nbsp;
we had made the short film actually people kept on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.080 --> 00:00:07.840
watching it and saying this should&nbsp;
actually be a television series.

00:00:07.840 --> 00:00:14.240
Film Courage: It's been what 11 years or so&nbsp;
since you started on this filmmaking journey?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.520 --> 00:00:19.360
Can you talk about the most amount of effort&nbsp;
that you've put toward making a feature&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.360 --> 00:00:23.627
and what have been the biggest challenges&nbsp;
for you and Sara [Sara O'Reilly].

00:00:23.627 --> 00:00:33.200
Jeremy: Yeah I think it's been 10-11 years&nbsp;
since we've really been actively making stuff.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.640 --> 00:00:45.600
The biggest effort we've put towards a&nbsp;
feature…well I have two two kind of thoughts about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.600 --> 00:00:52.960
that. The biggest effort we put towards the future&nbsp;
is a script that we had written two years ago&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.760 --> 00:00:59.920
that we called fender bender and it's the road&nbsp;
trip comedy the kind of film that we love.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.760 --> 00:01:08.160
We had produced a sizzle reel to really give&nbsp;
audiences or prospective investors producers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.360 --> 00:01:18.240
sort of a visual idea of what the film might&nbsp;
be and I think we'd we've gotten that script&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.240 --> 00:01:22.320
into the hands of a lot of production&nbsp;
companies (a lot of big production companies)&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.040 --> 00:01:34.000
and I’ve got to say all of them passed. There&nbsp;
wasn't one company that was like Oh let's pursue&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.000 --> 00:01:38.400
this. But just the fact that you're getting&nbsp;
them in there we were really excited about that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.200 --> 00:01:44.960
because that is like a positive sign that people&nbsp;
are liking it enough to start passing it around&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.960 --> 00:01:50.720
like it's not just going to those friends or&nbsp;
you know your your family or whatever but like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.720 --> 00:01:58.880
it's going to people who are moving the script&nbsp;
farther along and you know I feel like we had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.880 --> 00:02:05.600
a lot of momentum behind that project and then&nbsp;
of course the pandemic happened and so I think&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.720 --> 00:02:09.600
and not just us I think I think&nbsp;
there were a lot of people who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.880 --> 00:02:16.960
you know just just lost a little bit of that that&nbsp;
momentum and everything they were building towards&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.520 --> 00:02:23.280
so that was probably the hardest or the most&nbsp;
effort we've been put into a feature so far&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.920 --> 00:02:27.680
another thing that happened to us was&nbsp;
really interesting is that we had made&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.240 --> 00:02:35.440
a couple of years even before that we had made&nbsp;
another short a proof of concept short film&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.440 --> 00:02:42.000
that we were hoping to adapt into a feature&nbsp;
and you know we had the feature script ready&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.560 --> 00:02:49.520
and somebody had found our feature script an&nbsp;
actress had found our feature script you know a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:49.520 --> 00:02:56.480
prolific television actress you know had been&nbsp;
around and on shows that we all knew and she&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.040 --> 00:03:02.640
passed it to a producer and they actually&nbsp;
approached us about adapting it into a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.640 --> 00:03:08.320
television series which is something we've&nbsp;
never done and it's kind of interesting how&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.320 --> 00:03:13.360
these things sort of happen because while we&nbsp;
were after we had made the short film actually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.240 --> 00:03:20.480
people kept on watching it and saying this should&nbsp;
actually be a television series and Sara and I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:20.480 --> 00:03:23.440
were like oh yeah that's a you know that's a&nbsp;
good idea we hadn't really thought about that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.040 --> 00:03:30.080
but people kept on saying it people kept on saying&nbsp;
it and of course we were kind of locked into that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.640 --> 00:03:36.640
that you know that that narrow view that no it's&nbsp;
got to be a feature film you know we made it to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.640 --> 00:03:41.840
a proof of concept short and now it's got to&nbsp;
be a feature so we'd written it as feature but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:41.840 --> 00:03:49.200
then here were two people who wanted again we were&nbsp;
hearing to adapt it into television so we actually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.200 --> 00:03:56.800
signed a deal with them to adapt this short into&nbsp;
a television series and we spent about a year&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.800 --> 00:04:03.600
working on that and of course you know&nbsp;
as can happen so many times projects&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:03.600 --> 00:04:08.320
you know even though you're super excited&nbsp;
about them and you you really want them&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.320 --> 00:04:15.600
to go forward and there's a lot of momentum&nbsp;
behind it for whatever reason they they end&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:15.600 --> 00:04:22.400
up falling through and and things don't always&nbsp;
come to fruition the way you wished they would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:24.880 --> 00:04:31.760
but I'm using that example just to illustrate&nbsp;
like you never know how the project is going&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.760 --> 00:04:38.880
to come alive we had written a script wanted&nbsp;
to make it into a feature and then you know&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:38.880 --> 00:04:44.960
something happened that sort of you know set us&nbsp;
back but we had also had another script that we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:44.960 --> 00:04:50.720
wanted to make into a feature and we were making&nbsp;
that into a television series so I think you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:50.720 --> 00:04:56.720
don't really know how everything is going to fall&nbsp;
into place always so you just got to be ready to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:57.920 --> 00:05:08.480
kind of go wherever you know kind of go with your&nbsp;
best path forward and so going into something new&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:08.480 --> 00:05:14.800
do you always keep that in the back pocket of like&nbsp;
this this may don't get too excited I want to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:14.800 --> 00:05:20.480
on board and ready but it could fall&nbsp;
through and protecting yourself almost so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:22.800 --> 00:05:31.360
protecting yourself is I think absolutely crucial&nbsp;
I think again since I've done this for so long&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:31.360 --> 00:05:38.000
and since I've seen so many things throughout you&nbsp;
know my career and just the different jobs that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:38.000 --> 00:05:42.480
I've done and just different projects&nbsp;
that I've been involved with you know&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:43.440 --> 00:05:53.920
yes absolutely I think that's crucial that yes be&nbsp;
excited about something and and really want it to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:53.920 --> 00:06:00.640
happen and really do everything in your power to&nbsp;
make it happen but you know sometimes things will&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:01.520 --> 00:06:07.600
fall through and it's just completely out of&nbsp;
your control and and maybe sometimes it's not but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:08.480 --> 00:06:17.520
but I think keeping your expectations kind&nbsp;
of in check until I think until you're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:18.720 --> 00:06:24.160
I would even say you know this even goes back&nbsp;
to like my time as an actor you know actors can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:25.280 --> 00:06:29.360
book a really and this is this has happened to&nbsp;
me this has happened to so many friends that I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:29.360 --> 00:06:35.040
know you know you can book something really&nbsp;
amazing and you can shoot the part even you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:35.040 --> 00:06:40.320
know you can you can book it and you can be on set&nbsp;
and then when you're watching it you're cut out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:41.040 --> 00:06:47.840
you know and I think that same sort of mentality&nbsp;
must apply to the filmmaking side that like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:48.480 --> 00:06:55.520
I would never really consider something done&nbsp;
until I'm literally at home watching it or I'm&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:55.520 --> 00:07:01.840
in a movie theater watching it you know things&nbsp;
always happen you know best intentions I've seen&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:02.400 --> 00:07:07.120
people and friends make films we were talking&nbsp;
about this earlier sometimes it's taken these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:07.120 --> 00:07:12.080
people years but I've seen films that have&nbsp;
really and I've even been involved in films&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:12.080 --> 00:07:18.080
like that as an actor where people invest&nbsp;
so much time and so much money and the film&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:19.440 --> 00:07:26.480
never gets released ever people have never seen&nbsp;
it it's sitting on a shelf at somebody's house&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:26.480 --> 00:07:31.280
you know and it's really unfortunate when when&nbsp;
things like that do happen but they do happen so I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:31.280 --> 00:07:40.080
would say it is really crucial and maybe maybe you&nbsp;
know something you should start if people haven't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:40.080 --> 00:07:48.720
really worked on it or thought about that really&nbsp;
do kind of get yourself used to just things you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:48.720 --> 00:07:55.520
know falling through but everything will run its&nbsp;
course and I think one of the ways I've sort of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:57.840 --> 00:08:03.760
started to protect myself from that maybe is to&nbsp;
to we like to have just a couple things going on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:04.320 --> 00:08:10.960
you know maybe what's what's the what's the phrase&nbsp;
don't put all your eggs in one basket and I think&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:10.960 --> 00:08:15.760
for for me and for Sara and for our&nbsp;
production company that's been really&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:15.760 --> 00:08:23.520
helpful is that you know we're trying to just&nbsp;
have a few things that we're working on so that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:23.520 --> 00:08:28.800
if something does fall through you move on to&nbsp;
the next one and you're going with that one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:28.800 --> 00:08:32.960
and seeing where that one can go and then&nbsp;
maybe that one will fall through maybe not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:32.960 --> 00:08:40.080
and then if so you go into the next one but I&nbsp;
think that's really important but I think you know&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:40.080 --> 00:08:46.080
yeah when I if someone is just starting&nbsp;
out or or thinking of getting into this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:46.880 --> 00:08:51.760
it's gonna happen it's gonna happen you're&nbsp;
gonna you know things things fall through&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:52.960 --> 00:09:01.600
you don't seem jaded to me how do you keep&nbsp;
yourself upbeat very refreshing I think yeah&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:02.480 --> 00:09:07.840
not that I'm putting you on the spot or anything&nbsp;
but you seem very upbeat to me yeah I think um

00:09:11.760 --> 00:09:19.280
ah yeah how do I do it because it is true like&nbsp;
when when you get so attached to something&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:20.480 --> 00:09:26.240
it's difficult I mean I do I feel you know when&nbsp;
when when a project does fall through it's like ah&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:26.960 --> 00:09:31.520
you just feel like you were so you know you were&nbsp;
so close or you you know so many pieces were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:31.520 --> 00:09:37.120
falling into place and then this one thing sort of&nbsp;
like sucks the air out of it you know it is it's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:37.120 --> 00:09:45.680
it's really it can feel you feel terrible it's&nbsp;
it's you know it's really like deflating and and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:45.680 --> 00:09:56.560
you just it's like ugh again but I think you do&nbsp;
have to realize that you know it's it's and kind&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:56.560 --> 00:10:01.760
of we were talking about this earlier too I mean&nbsp;
it's a career it's like it's a the perseverance&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:01.760 --> 00:10:07.920
thing and it's kind of like you're in it for the&nbsp;
long haul at least that's how I've always felt and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:09.520 --> 00:10:14.480
and I think yeah you just you know it is good&nbsp;
to diversify yourself so you're not putting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:14.480 --> 00:10:19.840
all your eggs in one basket so if something falls&nbsp;
through you know that's it that's everything you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:19.840 --> 00:10:26.480
know you're out of la you're out of the business&nbsp;
I think you really do need to have a good attitude&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:26.480 --> 00:10:36.800
and and and just yeah be able to sort of overcome&nbsp;
it and yeah for me I think I think being able to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:38.400 --> 00:10:46.400
to have a career and to be able to I should say&nbsp;
something that's helped me has been being able&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:46.400 --> 00:10:52.000
to just always think about it that way I guess&nbsp;
to think about maintaining a a career not just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:52.960 --> 00:11:00.400
you know a one one hit or something&nbsp;
like that and I think yeah lately&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:01.280 --> 00:11:10.880
it really has felt for me a lot better having&nbsp;
multiple things going on so that if something&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:10.880 --> 00:11:27.840
does fall through because it will that I&nbsp;
can kind of put my focus on the next thing.

00:11:27.840 --> 00:11:33.237
Question For The Viewers: What has stopped&nbsp;
you from making your next big project?

